# mito-fuel — turn the glucose dial, watch the cascade (after mito-queue.html)
#
# mitochondria.strombild starts the proton story mid-stream, with the pump as a
# fixed inflow. But the pump is not fixed — it runs on glucose. This model puts
# the fuel dial back on the front and lets you watch one knob ripple down a chain
# of THREE stocks:
#
#     glucose  ──sets──>  the pump  ──fills──>  reservoir  ──drives──>  ATP
#     (the dial)                                (the reserve)           (production)
#
# glucose is a HELD node (no flow in or out — like french-flag's morphogen): it
# just sits at its level and is READ by the pump (of = glucose). Lower its init
# (or drag the dial) and the whole downstream chain re-settles lower — the pump
# slows, the reservoir reserve falls, ATP production falls with it. That is the
# point the book makes: glucose is the crude input; the gradient is a BUFFER that
# stores and meters it; ATP is the refined token. The reservoir cushions a brief
# glucose dip, but a sustained one starves production — you can read that lag in
# how the dam empties before the ATP bucket does.
#
# Two dashed influence arrows draw the causal chain: glucose governs the pump,
# the reservoir governs synthesis (read-not-drain, the café/french-flag move).
# Only the rotor drains the dam.
#
#   try it: init(glucose) = 70 → reservoir ~20, ATP ~24.  drop to 30 → reservoir
#   ~8.6, ATP ~10 — half the fuel, roughly half the reserve and half the output.
#
# THE NEXT GAP (mito-queue's real lesson): there, the motor needs BOTH the
# gradient AND ADP demand — production = gradient × demand, an AND-gate that
# stalls if either is missing. That is a rate reading TWO inputs at once, which a
# single edge cannot do; it wants the `reaction` recipe (the factory pattern) or
# the `limiting` bottleneck law. Left for the demand-coupled sequel.

node glucose   : range(0, 100), init(70)                          # the fuel dial — lower it, watch downstream
node reservoir : range(0, 100), init(20) @ bucket(fill = dots)    # the proton dam — the reserve
node atp       : range(0, 100), init(0)  @ bucket(fill = dots)    # the refined token — production
external etc        # electron transport chain — the pump (carriers from glucose drive it)
external matrix     # protons return, down the gradient
external adp        # ADP + Pi — empty tokens waiting to be charged
external work       # everything the cell does, paid for in ATP

etc       -> reservoir : proportional(of = glucose, k = 0.10)            # pump rate SET by glucose (read, not drained)
reservoir -> matrix    : proportional(of = reservoir, k = 0.35) @ rotor  # ATP synthase: the flux spins the rotor
adp       -> atp       : proportional(of = reservoir, k = 0.18)          # synthesis metered by the gradient
atp       -> work      : proportional(k = 0.15)                          # the cell spends it

pathway respiration : etc->reservoir, reservoir->matrix, adp->atp, atp->work
